nodecode-follow-through is a rewrite of Agenziamaia's Agenziamaia/follow-through:

  https://github.com/Agenziamaia/follow-through
  commit 9e4ddc528b62254c944ed25542902b6f99d652a4
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Giulio Di Stefano (agenziamaia) (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's one llm_execution middleware, its regexes
(__init__.py:30-91) and its follow_through tool, as one Nodecode cell. The
detector is the plugin's own, alternative for alternative, English and
Italian, with the same exemptions (a sentence shorter than twelve characters,
a question, a sentence that hands the next step to the person, a negated
clause) and the same rule for a quote of at most 300 characters. The tool
answers the same nudge text, the knob is the `follow_through' section's
`max_nudges' (the plugin's FOLLOW_THROUGH_MAX_NUDGES), and a tool call in
follow-through's shape is still offered under the plugin's name; the two
verbs the plugin had as an environment variable and a kill switch are
/follow-through status, on and off.

The mechanism had to change, and that is the one difference a user will
notice. Hermes lets a plugin rewrite the reply that was already written, so
the agent carries on inside the same turn. A Nodecode cell cannot rewrite a
finished answer; it reads the message as it is recorded (around
NLK:RECORD-ASSISTANT-MESSAGE, once per round of a live turn) and puts the
nudge into the session through NLE:SUBMIT, the one in-process ingress. The
turn that promised ends and the next one opens on the nudge, so the work
still really continues, but the nudge stands in the transcript as a tagged
prompt of its own. The count that capped the plugin at two nudges per user
turn is kept, now per prompt: a hook around NLE:SUBMIT starts it over at
every prompt that is not this cell's own, so a chain of promises cannot run
away and the next thing the person says resets it.

Left out, and why: the Tool Search bridge (`tool_call`), because whether a
plugin tool is deferred is a Hermes detail; FOLLOW_THROUGH_OFF, because
/follow-through off is the same switch and it is visible; and the plugin's
fail-open posture on an error is kept but is no longer something the reader
has to trust -- the nudge is given in NLE:SUBMIT, outside the promise check,
and a failure to give one leaves the turn untouched.
